  "verdict_reason": "Resolve the transaction, recovery and engine-boundary defects before dependent milestones freeze these interfaces.",
  "reply_markdown": "**Q1.** Keep the documented PS5.1 writer and accept both hosts’ JSON syntax. Mixed PS7/plugin writes will still cause formatting churn unless both writers agree. Preserve data before optimizing whitespace. Five existing compiled fixture tests passed; no fresh build ran. Unicode-key/exponent parity exceeds those fixtures (finding #16; `crates/c3-core/src/ps_json.rs:75`).\n\n**Q2.** Keep one attempt per engine call. Add `TurnKind`, explicit prompt delivery, and subprocess/HTTP plan variants; pass the full resolved request to secondary turns (findings #8–12; `crates/c3-core/src/engine.rs:138,186,299,361`). Muse’s guard must run immediately before every launch.\n\nRequired transitions: prepared→guarded→running. Guard refusal launches nothing; launch failure must report whether a child exists. Running→completed/failed/cancelled/timed-out preserves that attempt’s evidence. Only a main timeout, with termination confirmed and eligibility checks passed, may continue; survivors block it. Denied-empty agy output may retry once; usable output with a denial warning does not. Schema-invalid substantive output may receive one eligible repair; failed repair retains original prose. Failed secondary turns retain their own failure/salvage and cannot recursively trigger timeout continuation. These executions remain unimplemented.\n\n**Q3.** Runtime owns allocation, routing and transition policy; core should carry immutable identities/context. Retry keeps consultation, reviewer and inputs, allocates a new attempt, and never redraws. Fork/resume requires verified conversation provenance and matching provider/model/engine/endpoint; mismatch refuses launch. HTTP replay requires retained pack plus prior reply, with hash/lineage validation. Native resume and replay are separate capabilities (`docs/DESIGN.md:89`; findings #10–11).\n\n**Q4.** Keep the committed handoff authoritative; do not expand the ordered ledger for display prose. If recovery regenerates anything, retain the exact header or its versioned inputs. Introduce ordered optional-header slots and additional event links: today’s single `extra` slot cannot reproduce the plugin (finding #13; `crates/c3-core/src/handoff.rs:184`).\n\n**Q5.** Preserve conditional `Usage.total_tokens` (`crates/c3-core/src/ledger.rs:217`). Pin known non-null shapes now, while retaining unknown data losslessly. At plugin commit `9d79206`, `codex-consult.ps1:1107,2994,3197` defines range, denial-retry and timeout-continuation records. `peak` is boolean/null, not an object; `effort_confirmed` remains null-only evidence. Raw JSON is acceptable storage, insufficient specification for new writers.\n\n**Q6.** Confirm the compatible placement: versioned pack reference/hash in `reviewer.provider_config`, backed by retained `.pack.md/.pack.json`; no ledger column or reply-schema bump. However, DESIGN explicitly mandates sidecars and evidence references, not that provider-config placement (`docs/DESIGN.md:95`; `docs/port/contracts.md:135`). Persist provenance before sessions commit; keep endpoint identity independent of per-consultation pack metadata.\n\n**Q7.** OS locking/backoff may live in runtime, but the transaction interface must require ownership. Current writable `FilesStore` does not enforce it and replaces stale findings (findings #1–2; `crates/c3-core/src/store.rs:330,358`).\n\nLock states: free→held→released; acquisition failure changes no stores, process death releases ownership. Pending: reserved→launching→running; failed registration retains launching, failed termination retains survivors, completed work→committing. Reservation-write failure must prevent launch. Every secondary launch repeats registration safeguards. Raw reply precedes locking; under lock re-read/apply delta→handoff→findings→sessions→cleanup. Lock timeout or pre-session write failure retains recovery; findings-before-sessions failure leaves an orphan. Sessions success is committed even if cleanup fails; live-process evidence must remain. Current panel addressing, survivor cleanup and commit-result semantics violate these boundaries (findings #3–5; `crates/c3-core/src/store.rs:351,392`).\n\n**Q8.** Freeze legacy/additive preservation, validated replies, worktree identity, contained Windows paths and cancellation ownership before adapters depend on them (findings #6–7,#11–12,#14–15,#17). R12 should remain a versioned, derived status snapshot with consultation/attempt/member identities; its final wire shape is unproven.\n\nFinding states are proposed, implemented, verified, rejected, wontfix, superseded. Every pair is allowed; verified requires evidence, rejected requires note, reopening requires note. Failed gates mutate nothing. The pure validator does not append history: runtime must atomically apply status plus append-only history, keeping reviewer checks separate (`crates/c3-core/src/findings.rs:32,174`). Evidence does not demonstrate persistence of these transitions.\n\n## Requested checks\n\n- **RC1:** CWD repository root; workspace-write; execute a disposable store fault-injection procedure with two concurrent writers and interruption at each commit step. Require preserved deltas, member records, survivor evidence and unambiguous commit status (findings #1–7). Budget: 12 cases.\n- **RC2:** CWD repository root; workspace-write; add and run focused engine contract tests for findings #8–12,#17 using `cargo test -p c3-core --locked`. Require exact argv, explicit turn context/outcomes and invalid-reply rejection. Budget: 30 cases.\n- **RC3:** CWD repository root; workspace-write; differential-round-trip legacy/extended stores, optional headers and Unicode/exponent inputs against plugin `9d79206`/PS5.1. Require lossless data and declared formatting parity (findings #6–7,#13,#16). Budget: 20 fixtures.",
